  "query_preview": "/Through Saturday/...\nIssued at 1249 PM MDT Sat Aug 22 2026\n\nSatellite and radar shows pretty healthy convection developing\nacross the Front Range Mountains and Foothills, while the airmass\nis still somewhat stable (per ACARS and SPC analysis) but \nincreasingly moist over the plains. The increased moisture on the \nplains is being reinforced by a couple westerly moving surges, \nand dewpoints have held in the upper 50s and lower 60s east and \nnorth of Denver. The latest surge coming back through Elbert \nCounty will likely boost values over the Palmer Divide this \nafternoon, especially as we develop a surface cyclone in vicinity \nof Douglas or western Elbert County. That will aid moisture \nconvergence and potential for convective initiation there, along \nwith a risk of a landspout. Otherwise, stronger storms today will \nbe capable of hail and strong winds, along with locally heavy \nrainfall as precipitable water (PW) values climb to 150-170% of \nnormal and low level moisture remains abundant over the plains. As\nper typical, we still think the highest threat of strongest \nstorms would be along/east of the convergence zone associated with\nthe developing cyclone and points northward along/just east of \nI-25 - essentially covering all of the plains. A couple severe \nstorms are likely due to MLCAPE increasing to 1200-2000 J/kg. Bulk\nshear is weak and less than 25-30 kts, however, so majority of \nthe severe storms would tend to be pulse (brief). Storm motion \nwill be rather slow at 15 mph or less given lack of flow aloft, \nwhich will lead to at least some minor flooding threats in a few \nspots. Overall progression will eventually be to the east across \nthe plains into late evening, with potential for a generally \nweaker subsequent batch or two of storms behind the initial \nstronger ones that would have access to more instability. \n\nWe still expect some gradual clearing overnight, but patchy\nstratus/fog possible across the plains given the very moist\nairmass.  \n\nOn Sunday, there is some drying noted as flow aloft turns a bit\nmore westerly. However, we're not convinced we'll erode all the \nlow level moisture across the plains. Even in the mountains we'll \nstill see enough to spark scattered thunderstorms, and then those \nstorms will likely intensify as they move onto the plains as they \nencounter a richer, more unstable airmass. Bulk shear profiles are\nstill marginal, but a couple severe storms will be possible given\nsufficient MLCAPE of 1000-1800 J/kg. Similar weather conditions \nwill likely persist into Monday with little change in the overall \npattern, moisture, and instability profiles. Temperatures will be \nrather warm yet, with highs in the 90-95 degree range over the \nI-25 Corridor and plains. \n\nThere is better agreement of a weak backdoor cool front or two for\nTuesday and Wednesday, offering up a few degrees of cooling and\npotentially more active weather over the Front Range and plains.\n\nThursday and Friday are shaping up to be transition days into a\ndrier and warmer environment with progressively less storm\ncoverage, especially by Friday.  \n\nEnsembles are consistent showing a return of summer heat for next\nweekend, with highs pushing into the mid 90s (30% chance of upper\n90s) for the I-25 Corridor and plains as a stronger zonal flow \naloft develops. That would also lead to increasing fire danger as \nwe begin our journey toward autumn breezes. Unfortunately, \ntemperatures will feel more like mid summer and humidities will \ndrop in the drier westerly flow.",
